{"name":"Saint Seiya Gold","hex":"#eeee00","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"saint","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deceased person whom a church or another religious group has officially recognised as especially holy or godly; one eminent for piety and virtue.","One of the blessed in heaven."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Synonym of canonize: to honor, formally name, or revere as a saint."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/saint"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Saint Seiya","blocks":[{"text":"Saint Seiya , also known as Saint Seiya: Knights of the Zodiac or simply Knights of the Zodiac, is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Masami Kurumada. It was serialized in Shueisha's shōnen manga magazine Weekly Shōnen Jump from 1985 to 1990, with its chapters collected in 28 tankōbon volumes. In North America, the manga was licensed for English release by Viz Media."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/75/Saint_Seiya_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Seiya","wikidata":"Q275956"},{"title":"Saint","blocks":[{"text":"In Christian belief, a saint, also known as a hallow, is a person who is recognized as having an exceptional degree of holiness, likeness, or closeness to God. However, the use of the term saint depends on the context and denomination. Official ecclesiastical recognition, and veneration, is conferred on some denominational saints through the process of canonization in the Catholic Church or glorification in the Eastern Orthodox Church after their approval. The saints are honored in the liturgical calendars of Evangelical Lutheranism and Anglicanism. In other nonconformist denominations, such as the Plymouth Brethren, and following from Pauline usage, saint refers broadly to any holy Christian without special recognition or selection."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Greek_School_-_Icon_of_Saint_John_the_Baptist_-_1102557_-_National_Trust.jpg/330px-Greek_School_-_Icon_of_Saint_John_the_Baptist_-_1102557_-_National_Trust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint","wikidata":"Q43115"},{"title":"Seiya","blocks":[{"text":"Seiya (星矢) is a masculine name of Japanese origin. It is a common masculine Japanese given name."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seiya","wikidata":"Q25113718"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Saint_Peter%27s_Basilica_facade%2C_Rome%2C_Italy.jpg/500px-Saint_Peter%27s_Basilica_facade%2C_Rome%2C_Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Saint_Peter%27s_Basilica_facade%2C_Rome%2C_Italy.jpg/1280px-Saint_Peter%27s_Basilica_facade%2C_Rome%2C_Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa